rgence of those two things it's a  love of fantasy that I got for my mom I  love the transcendent art of Japan that  I got for my dad all bound together in a  film that's about the sustaining love of  family so it's a film inspired by family  about family as you guys now are using  stop-motion but also CGI and and and  computer-generated animation how do you  say okay this is going to be this or  that's going to be that and how much of  that shifts throughout production and  how do you keep your thumb on the budget  and the time and yeah how do you do what  you do it's a good question I think we  were as a studio not really ready for  this movie until now so  so we've been here for ten years all of  these incredible people have been here  you know all along and I think Travis  especially was looking for a really big  epic action adventure something we could  sink our teeth into and it wasn't until  maybe five years ago that we're ready to  do it and so what was there were a lot  of things that were really worrying some  about it it was a big outdoor action  adventure with this huge scale and of  course we shoot on you know tabletops  like desks inside a warehouse in the in  the dark so that was challenging there's  tons of water there's different kinds of  characters that we hadn't tackled before  but we have learned so much through the  different kinds of movies that we've  done before and every the whole group  you know of creative collaborators are  really here with us this whole time so  once we learn one thing okay we know  that we can put that into our library of  things that we know how to do and we can  tackle something new and so at that  point we were ready but terrified how do  we decide to shoot in the camera I think  we always want to shoot in camera as  much as we can  because it will look whole it'll look  like a movie to look you know more  beautiful and you know as you travel  through a movie like this where you've  got like 80 different locations maybe  you want to make sure that it looks  apart you know that the world looks  really whole at made of one hand and I  think we try to do everything in camera  and that which we cannot do in camera we  turn to our visual effects Department  our visual effects Department is like  these guys here with us at the very  beginning it's not something that we  decide to do at the end of the movie  like a post-production thing you know  afterthought they work and collaborate  with these guys with all of us from the  beginning and so we're more you know  easily able to tackle things and we also  do things really differently it's it's  not like it's not necessarily  traditional the way you might do it  movie so we have a few tricks up our  sleeves Georgia puppet fabrications  supervisor I'm assuming that means urine  you supervise all the manufacturing of  the puppets how do you even begin to  figure out what the materials are that  you're going to use it I mean it all  starts at the beginning of the film so  we have an amazing production designer  who is researching the look of the  entire movie and actually a lot of the  influence across the board of the movie  was taken from origami paper folding the  simple graphic lines of paper folding so  when it came down to us having to make  puppets out of origami we did the same  thing we went to another level of  research I had a team a small team of  people who had been making stop-motion  traditional puppets up until that point  but we had a lot of visual reference we  tackled all of the characters firstly in  traditional origami we had a woman that  came in and she did all of the maquette  in paper in origami and it was then our  job to work out how on earth we were  going to make this you know able to  withstand an animator touching it let  alone animating it for a year so we went  through material development and we also  sort of went through deconstructing  paper folding origami techniques to then  reconstruct them into a moveable  workable puppet so each of our origami  characters had metal armatures and  underneath the paper that it was a  combination of hard parts which were  covered in in the paper and I say paper  we couldn't use paper the first puppet  that we've sent out there with a little  bit of paper on just tore up instantly  so we actually ended up using Tyvek  which you know commonly in the building  trade and in the suits that people go  into laboratories in it looks like paper  but it has this amazing tear strength  you know you can't tear it  you also can't paint it very easily so  that was a bit of you know the thing  with puppet making the fun of it is you  have a problem put in front of you or  you know a creative challenge and that's  what it's all about how are we going to  figure this out and visual effects  helped us well but only to a small  degree you know a lot  what you're seeing out there it's  practical and it was a lot of the  animators informing us what they needed  to turn a flat piece of paper into a  three-dimensional origami character  fantastic and I assume that you must  have worked very closely with Deborah  because at the same time that you're  creating these puppets which seems like  too simple a word to be using sorry but  how do you begin to be a figure out not  only what these characters should look  like but whether the practicality of  that is going to work out there's a lot  of research done beginning on the  engineer and if the costumes as much as  the history of the character and the  history of the film the Japanese history  of the film that we looked into as well  but following on from what George said  about origami in forming a lot of the  characters we also use that as a jumping  off point for how the costumes were  going to work as the kimonos are mainly  flat wide shapes that are just wrapped  and tied to bodies and they're not  tailored which we've been used to making  they're used to being fitted to the  armature and how do we control all of  those folds so looking at origami just  as a jumping-off point but also looking  at EC Miyagi's designs who created  folded clothing that just is very  elegant over a body and follows the form  of a body using those as inspiration  it's how we were going to make those  graphic lines a language for the film  that travel between the characters so a  lot of our costumes now have their own  engineering underneath and they pretty  much have their own armatures at this  point especially in this film and  especially with the sisters character to  control those folks there's a lot of  engineering underneath that we've  developed over the course of time in our  costume making for like where we learn  learn one thing or trick because you  might want to call it on on one film and  one costume but we can reinterpret that  and use that in a different area on  others so it's a combination of those  things so there's several materials  under those costumes including Tyvek to  make the wrinkled surface they have  weighted linings made with tungsten the  nickel  to unglue this laser-cut to create the  gravity in the scale and the kind of  dimpled surface of old silk and then  armed armatures to control the flow of  the sleeves so Brian director of rapid  prototyping I love that title I don't  mean to make fun of it at all I thought  sure that there was a lot of thought  into coming up with that title so  explain first of all what that is and  how that interfaces with you know for  the further layman so basically what we  do it like for facial animation is we  are combining about a hundred-year-old  technique of replacement animation with  21st century 3d printing technology this  is something we started over a decade  ago on Coraline and we were the first  studio to ever try this crazy idea and  essentially what it is is instead of  hand sculpting different facial  expressions that an animator would then  be putting on a puppet in taking a frame  of film and then replacing that  expression with another hand sculpted  face we thought could we actually do  that in the computer probably one of the  best examples of replacement animation  before Coraline would have been Jack  Skellington in Nightmare Before  Christmas and he had about 800 facial  expressions for the entire film so our  original goal was hey we're gonna be  able to produce 800 faces pretty quick  using this 3d printer so back on  Coraline we were producing these faces  out of a plastic printer that then we  needed to go in and actually  individually hand paint every single  face so we were really pushing the  performance of these characters to  unseen areas but we were still really  having to dial back the sophistication  of how their paint jobs could look on  ParaNorman we started looking at  different types of 3d printing  technology which is started looking at  color 3d printed technology and we were  using that and have used that since  ParaNorman boxtrolls and we use that for  the human characters on Kubo but one of  the downsides to this powder color  printing that we were using it was  really hard to get fine feature detail  so when we were seeing the early on  character designs for kuba on the two  strings there were three characters in  particular that we're very  nerve-wracking for  so the monkey character with her fur  beetle with his you know hard facets on  his face and then the the moon Bea's  character at the end that serpent and it  was the first time in my career that I  we were at a crossroads that I either  had to go back to the director and say  hey our technology that we're using  isn't going to be able to complete your  vision or do we find a new way to do it  and the amazing thing about the last 10  years of like it and the rapid  prototyping process is not only had we  made a name for ourselves in the 3d  printing world Arches me in the  animation world we'd also made a pretty  name pretty good name for ourselves in  the 3d printing world we had an  opportunity to reach out to some of  these companies and say alright what do  you guys have in beta testing what do  you guys have in in machines that you  haven't released to the public yet is  there any way that we can actually tap  into that and see if that would help us  help deliver Travis's look for moon  beast and monkey and beetle and it was a  perfect scenario that where there was a  plastic 3d printer that was in the works  with a company called Stratasys we had  an opportunity to go to this 3d printing  company and say hey we love your  hardware but let us develop the software  let us be the ones who you know figure  out how we can take a face put paint on  it put three-dimensional paint on it and  then send it to one of these plastic  printers and it worked out really well  it was not without a lot of hard work  from a lot of very dedicated people that  we were basically trailblazing a  completely new way of producing plastic  color printed faces thank you very much  and thank you for saying

